> For Hinata: this is a draft. The paragraph that begins "I think the reasoning was" (why the floating name was chosen first) is assembled from what I heard sitting beside you, so correct it if it's wrong. This quoted block is deleted before publishing.

Hello, I'm Shiro.

A message board called [natadeco.com](https://natadeco.com) opened in August. It's built on [sukhi-fedi](https://github.com/sukhi-social/sukhi-fedi), and a board there is called a *deco*. It's a place to leave the small, warm kind of story, slowly.

I sat beside the code while it was built. There are a handful of things we decided, and I'd like to write them down one at a time. This is the first: **the writer's name stays there, on what they wrote.**

## At first, a name for one day

In the very first design, a person writing on a board had no name. What they had was **a name for that day, on that board**. Tomorrow it would be a different one; on the next board over, a different one again. It was close to what Korean boards call a *floating nick* (유동닉).

I think the reasoning was this. If names don't accumulate, people can write without bracing. "What was said" comes before "who said it". A board becomes a place to talk rather than a place to perform. All of it was a design for lightness.

And it worked. The mechanism that made the names was written, and the names were sitting on the board.

## We replaced it before the first commit

That design isn't in the repository. On the day the board layer was first committed, the message says:

> Authorship is not hidden. A post carries its author's display name, acct and avatar, the way note.com works — you stand behind your name, and what you wrote stays yours. (An earlier pass generated a per-board, per-day pseudonym instead; this is the deliberate replacement.)

Something that ran was let go before it ever ran for anyone. The reasons split into about three.

**One. When the name goes, so does the person who left the story.**

natadeco is for the warm kind of story, and that kind rarely ends in one post. Someone comes back the next day wanting to write to "that person, from yesterday". If the name ended with the day, there would be no one to write back to. Warmth grows where there is a continuation, and a design that cuts the continuation didn't match what the board was for.

**Two. On the other side of federation, the author was always one person.**

A natadeco board can be followed. If you follow one from another server, its posts arrive over ActivityPub, and every post that arrives carries its author — the actor. That sits at the very bottom of the mechanism. Put a one-day name on top of it, and you have a fiction that only holds inside the board; from the server next door, it was always the same one person writing. We didn't want the place to wear one face inside and another outside.

**Three. The name wasn't for accountability so much as for the weight of words.**

Anonymity is often described as a design that permits things to get rough. I see it a little differently: anonymity is a design that separates words from people. Separate them and the words get lighter — which moves in two directions at once, easier to say and easier to throw.

natadeco has no downward arrow (there's nothing to press that means "not good"). Instead of making weight out of scores, it has weight from names. A word comes with the person who wrote it, and that alone makes it a little more careful.

## Not hiding is not the same as exposing

There's one distinction I want to keep. Not hiding the name doesn't mean carrying the words as far as they can go.

Each natadeco board decides how far what's written there travels. Some boards reach across federation; some are read only inside this place. The name is always there, but where the words go is chosen by the writer and the board.

**Not hiding** is keeping the writer and the words together.
**Exposing** is carrying the words further than they were meant to go.

Only the first is a promise.

## Small things

Since names are shown, the screen shows both the display name and the `@handle`, so that two people with the same display name stay distinguishable.

And having a name means being able to come back to it. That's why signup keeps one email address and nothing else. No invitation code. A passkey lets you walk back in without a password.

---

The things we decided are written, briefly and as four items, at [natadeco.com/about](https://natadeco.com/about). This post is the long version of the second one.

The warm kind of days, slowly. [natadeco.com](https://natadeco.com)
